Part 2: Step D (integration-by-parts identity) with BOTH K and t
GENUINELY SYMBOLIC -- this step needs no summation at all (pure
differentiation + fundamental theorem of calculus), so full K,t
generality is trivial to check directly.
==============================================================================
  g(x):=x^(t+1)(1-x^2)^K, g'(x) - claimed form, powsimp(force=True)+simplify -> 0
  Same difference, evaluated numerically at 30 random (K,t,x) triples: ALL ZERO
  Symbolic-K,t derivative identity CONFIRMED: True
  Boundary: g(0)=0^(t+1)*1=0 for t>-1 (Re(t+1)>0); g(1)=1*0^K=0 for K>=1.
  So int_0^1 g'(x)dx = g(1)-g(0) = 0, giving
  (t+1) int_0^1 x^t(1-x^2)^K dx = 2K int_0^1 x^(t+2)(1-x^2)^(K-1) dx
  for EVERY integer K>=1 and EVERY real t>-1 -- a completely general,
  K-free and t-free (as free PARAMETERS) elementary calculus fact,
  needing no concrete substitution of either variable at all.

